PLACES=[
N('willamette-falls-locks','Willamette Falls Locks','West Linn, Oregon','or','canal','standing',45.35444,-122.61882,'area','view','drive','canal','1873–2011',
  'Four linked locks beside Willamette Falls, built from 1870 to 1873 for a private canal company so boats could pass the falls. The U.S. Army Corps of Engineers bought the canal and locks in 1915 and passed boats through free. They closed in 2011 and the lock system has been in "caretaker" status since. Wikipedia says repair work began in 2023 and the locks were expected to reopen in 2026; whether they have is not checked. Listed on the National Register in 1974.',
  'The four chambers of masonry and concrete with their gates, on the west side of the river across from Oregon City. Wikipedia says the stonework is local basalt and that part of the canal wall was timber filled with stone. The locks and their small park and museum have been closed to the public since 2011.',
  'Boats climbed 50 feet here for 138 years.',
  'The locks are federal property run by the Corps of Engineers; the Willamette Falls Locks Commission (willamettefallslocks.org) says the locks, park and museum closed in December 2011 and are in caretaker status. Look from public land only; do not enter the works. Parking not checked.',
  [W('Willamette Falls Locks'),['Willamette Falls Locks Commission: about the locks','https://www.willamettefallslocks.org/about']],
  conf='mixed',brick='?'),
N('aurora-colony-historic-district','Aurora Colony Historic District','Aurora, Oregon','or','town','standing',45.23154,-122.75398,'site','open','drive','','1856–1883',
  'A Christian communal society founded in 1856 by the Prussian-born William Keil, who had led an earlier colony at Bethel, Missouri. At its peak in 1868 it had about 600 people and 15,000 acres, and built homes, schools, mills, businesses and a hotel. Keil began handing land to families in 1872, and the colony was formally dissolved in 1883. About 150 acres and 12 buildings were listed on the National Register in 1974. The Old Aurora Colony Museum was founded in 1966.',
  'Colony-era buildings in and around the village, with the museum in the middle. What each building holds, and which are open beyond the museum, is not checked. The pin is on the museum, in the middle of the district.',
  'A German communal village from 1856, with its museum.',
  'The Old Aurora Colony Museum is at 15018 2nd Street NE (museum page: guided tours $10 a person). Other colony buildings are mostly in use; look from the street. Parking not checked.',
  [W('Aurora Colony'),['Old Aurora Colony Museum: hours and directions','https://auroracolony.org/hours-directions']],
  conf='mixed',brick='?',hours='Museum Monday to Saturday 10 a.m. to 5 p.m., Sunday noon to 5 p.m.; closed Thanksgiving, December 25 and January 1'),
N('yaquina-bay-lighthouse','Yaquina Bay Lighthouse','Newport, Oregon','or','light','standing',44.62415,-124.0629,'exact','open','drive','','1871–1874',
  'A wooden lighthouse and keeper\'s house in one building, built by Ben Simpson and first lit on November 3, 1871. It served only three years: the larger Yaquina Head light, three miles north, replaced it and it was decommissioned on October 1, 1874. Later the Army Corps of Engineers lived in it while building the jetties (1888 to 1896) and the Coast Guard used it from 1906 to 1915. It was a county museum for 18 years to 1970, and was listed on the National Register in 1970 and restored in 1974.',
  'The lighthouse itself, one of few built with the light on top of the keeper\'s dwelling, and the eight-story Coast Guard lookout tower beside it, in a state park above the bay. Wikipedia says the lighthouse shows a fixed white light again and is open for tours daily. What is inside was not checked.',
  'A lighthouse that was already out of date three years after it was lit.',
  'Yaquina Bay State Recreation Site, Oregon Parks and Recreation Department; park office 541-265-4560 (park page). Hours were not read at the source: a search result quoting Oregon State Parks material from 2019 said open seven days a week, with summer hours of 11 a.m. to 4 p.m. and shorter hours the rest of the year; a Travel Oregon page quoted in the same search gave Wednesday to Sunday noon to 4 p.m. Call before going.',
  [W('Yaquina Bay Light'),['Oregon State Parks: Yaquina Bay',OP%'157']],
  conf='mixed',brick='?',hours='?'),
N('coquille-river-lighthouse','Coquille River Lighthouse','Bandon, Oregon','or','light','standing',43.12391,-124.42422,'exact','public','drive','','1896–1939',
  'A lighthouse at the mouth of the Coquille River, first lit on February 29, 1896 as Bandon Light, to guide ships over the shifting bar. Bandon burned in a wildfire in September 1936 and its shipping declined; the light was shut down in 1939 and replaced by an automatic one on the south jetty. After 37 years of neglect and vandalism it was first restored in 1976, but the keeper\'s quarters and outbuildings were past repair and were removed. A solar light was put in the tower in 1991.',
  'The small tower at the end of the river jetty. Wikipedia says the lower level is open to the public in summer, while the tower, spiral stair and balcony are closed for safety. Fresnel lens and fog siren were removed long ago.',
  'The lighthouse that outlived the town it served.',
  'Bullards Beach State Park, Oregon Parks and Recreation Department. Hours for the lower level were not read at the source: the summer opening is Wikipedia\'s statement only. Parking not checked.',
  [W('Coquille River Light'),['Oregon State Parks: Bullards Beach','https://stateparks.oregon.gov/index.cfm?do=park.profile&parkId=137']],
  conf='mixed',brick='?'),
N('fort-klamath-site','Fort Klamath Site','Fort Klamath, Oregon','or','fort','traces',42.69194,-121.97222,'site','open','drive','forts','1863–1890',
  'An army post set up in 1863 between what is now Crater Lake National Park and Upper Klamath Lake. It had more than 50 buildings and a sawmill and was important in conflicts with the Klamath, Modoc and Northern Paiute. Four Modoc men led by Kintpuash were hanged here in 1873 for the killing of General Edward Canby; their graves remain. The troops left on June 23, 1890. The site is on the National Register.',
  'An 8-acre county park and museum within the old fort area. The museum is a modern building shaped like the fort\'s guardhouse and standing where the guardhouse stood. Little of the original fort is described by Wikipedia beyond the Modoc graves; what else can be seen is not checked.',
  'A Modoc War fort whose guardhouse is now a museum.',
  'Run by Klamath County museums, a seasonal museum. A search result quoting the county museum page said 10 a.m. to 6 p.m., Thursday to Monday, June to September; an older press release said Thursday to Tuesday, so days are not settled. Call 541-882-1000 before going.',
  [W('Fort Klamath')],
  conf='mixed',brick='?',hours='?'),
N('fort-rock-homestead-village','Fort Rock Homestead Village','Fort Rock, Oregon','or','town','standing',43.3556,-121.0585,'site','open','drive','','1900s–1988',
  'An open-air village of original homestead-era buildings of the early 1900s, moved here from abandoned claims around the Fort Rock Valley and opened as a museum in 1988 with two buildings. It has a church, a school, houses, cabins and other buildings. Homesteaders poured into the valley from 1905 to about 1915 and many small towns grew up; the high desert defeated most of them.',
  'Buildings furnished with homesteaders\' furniture, dishes and tools, and a community garden. Wikipedia says it is open for self-guided walking tours; its opening days differ between its own paragraphs.',
  'A village of homesteaders\' buildings rescued from the high desert.',
  'Run by the Fort Rock Valley Historical Society, 64696 Fort Rock Road, at the road junction in Fort Rock; 541-576-2251. A search result quoting the society\'s own page for 2026 gave Thursday to Sunday, 11 a.m. to 5 p.m. (last entry 4:30), from May 21 to the second weekend of September, adults $6; the page itself could not be opened here.',
  [W('Fort Rock Valley Historical Homestead Museum'),['Fort Rock Valley Historical Society','https://fortrockoregon.com/']],
  conf='mixed',brick='?',hours='Thursday to Sunday 11 a.m. to 5 p.m. (last entry 4:30), late May to mid-September; as quoted from the society\'s page'),
N('philip-foster-farm','Philip Foster Farm','Eagle Creek, Oregon','or','landmark','standing',45.35876,-122.35345,'site','open','drive','','1847',
  'Part of a 640-acre claim bought in 1847 by the pioneer Philip Foster, on the last leg of the Barlow Road, which emigrants took around Mount Hood. Foster built a store, a house, a barn and other buildings. The farmhouse and barn still stand; the store, blacksmith shop and log cabin on the site are replicas. It was listed on the National Register in 1980.',
  'The farmhouse and barn, with replica buildings and signs. The farm\'s own page offers guided tours and costumed events; what original fittings survive inside was not checked.',
  'The "first destination resort in the Oregon Territory", on the Oregon Trail.',
  'Owned by the Jacknife Zion Horse Heaven Historical Society. Its page says the grounds are open dawn to dusk daily and drop-in guided tours run on Saturdays May to October, $5 a person. Parking not checked.',
  [W('Philip Foster Farm'),['Philip Foster Farm','https://philipfosterfarm.com']],
  conf='mixed',brick='?',hours='Grounds dawn to dusk daily; guided tours Saturdays 11 a.m. to 4 p.m., May to October ($5)'),
N('thomas-kay-woolen-mill-salem','Thomas Kay Woolen Mill (Willamette Heritage Center)','Salem, Oregon','or','industry','standing',44.935,-123.02694,'site','open','drive','','1889–1962',
  'A woolen mill begun in 1889 by Thomas Lister Kay, whose descendants later founded Pendleton Woolen Mills. A fire destroyed the first mill in 1895 and a new one went up on the same spot; the brick building standing today opened on May 15, 1896. It is now part of a five-acre museum, the Willamette Heritage Center, formed in 2010 from two older societies.',
  'The brick mill with its machinery exhibits and a water-power display, plus relocated National Register buildings on the site: the Jason Lee House, the Methodist Parsonage, the John D. Boon House and the Pleasant Grove church, restored to a mid-1800s look. The museum says it shows nine historic buildings.',
  'A brick woolen mill and four moved pioneer buildings in one yard.',
  'Willamette Heritage Center, 1313 Mill Street SE. Its page says self-guided tours are Tuesday to Saturday, 10 a.m. to 4 p.m., and docent tours need two weeks\' notice. Free admission for qualifying visitors (Museums for All). Parking not checked.',
  [W('Willamette Heritage Center'),['Willamette Heritage Center: visit','https://willametteheritage.org/visit']],
  conf='mixed',brick='yes',hours='Self-guided tours Tuesday to Saturday 10 a.m. to 4 p.m.'),
N('flavel-house-astoria','Captain George Flavel House','Astoria, Oregon','or','landmark','standing',46.18806,-123.835,'exact','open','drive','','1885',
  'A Queen Anne house of 1885, built by George Flavel, a Columbia River bar pilot who became one of the area\'s first millionaires. It fills a whole city block, about 11,600 square feet. His wife and daughters lived on in it until the last died in 1933. It was listed on the National Register in 1980 with its carriage house, and is now a museum run by the Clatsop County Historical Society.',
  'The house and carriage house, furnished as a museum. Wikipedia says it appears as the museum in the film The Goonies. Original woodwork and fittings inside were not checked.',
  'A sea pilot\'s Queen Anne mansion, and the museum in The Goonies.',
  'Clatsop County Historical Society, 441 8th Street, tickets at the Carriage House. Its own page returned an error here. Aggregator listings quoted in a search said daily 10 a.m. to 4 p.m. October to April and to 5 p.m. May to September, adults $7; these are not from the society, so hours are not checked.',
  [W('Captain George Flavel House Museum')],
  conf='mixed',brick='?',hours='?'),
N('reid-school-bend','Reid School (Deschutes Historical Museum)','Bend, Oregon','or','civic','standing',44.0553,-121.3171,'exact','open','drive','','1914',
  'Built in 1914 as the first modern school building in Bend, replacing a three-story wooden school of 1904 (the first schoolhouse had opened in 1887). It stayed in the school district until 1979, when the county took it over for a local history museum, which opened in 1980 and is run by the Deschutes County Historical Society.',
  'The school building, now holding exhibits on Central Oregon prehistory, tribes, pioneers, logging and the Forest Service, with a history library and bookstore. What of the schoolrooms survives was not checked.',
  'Bend\'s first modern school, now its history museum.',
  'Deschutes Historical Museum, 129 NW Idaho Avenue, 541-389-1813. Wikipedia says it is closed Sundays and Mondays; its own pages gave no hours when read here, and search-result listings disagree, so hours are not checked. Admission on its Visit Us page, as quoted by search: $10, seniors $8, 17 and under free.',
  [W('Reid School (Bend, Oregon)'),W('Deschutes Historical Museum'),['Deschutes Historical Museum','https://deschuteshistory.org/visit-us']],
  conf='mixed',brick='?',hours='?'),
]
